 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Whether your kids should skip school or weekend activities in Kuala Lumpur comes down to their personal health and your comfort level, as the **Ministry of Education (MOE) explicitly allows parents to keep their children at home** during the haze if they are concerned for their health.\n\nTo help you make the decision, consider the current air quality, official policies, and health factors:\n\n### 1. Current Air Quality in KL\n* **API Status:** Air Pollutant Index (API) readings in Kuala Lumpur (such as in Cheras and Batu Muda) and surrounding Klang Valley areas are hovering in the **Unhealthy range (roughly 160 to 170+)**. \n* *(Note: Verify the exact hourly reading for your specific neighborhood on the official DOE website at **eqms.doe.gov.my** or via the **MyIPU** app).*\n\n### 2. Official Ministry of Education (MOE) Guidelines\nFollowing policy updates from Education Minister Fadhlina Sidek, the MOE has reverted to the National Haze Action Plan (PTJK) guidelines:\n* **API over 100:** All outdoor school activities, sports, and Co-Curricular Activities (CCA) are strictly suspended. (Since today is Saturday, if your kids were going in for outdoor sports or extra classes, those should already be canceled or moved indoors).\n* **API over 200:** Schools are mandated to close immediately and shift to home-based learning (PdPR). Because KL's reading is below 200, schools in KL are not under a mandatory closure order (unlike several districts in Selangor that closed recently).\n* **Parental Discretion:** The MOE has officially stated that **parents have the right to keep their children home** if they fear the haze will affect their health, even if the API hasn't hit 200. You just need to formally notify the school administration or class teacher so their absence is excused.\n\n### 3. Should You Keep Them Home?\n**You should strongly consider keeping them home if:**\n* **They have pre-existing conditions:** If your children have asthma, allergies, sinus issues, or any respiratory sensitivities, an API of 160+ can easily trigger an attack.\n* **The school is not enclosed:** Most Malaysian public schools have open-air classrooms with fans rather than sealed, air-conditioned rooms with air purifiers. If they will be sitting in open air all day, their exposure will be high.\n* **They walk or take open transport:** If their commute involves walking or waiting outdoors for buses, their exposure increases significantly.\n\n**If you do send them to school:**\n* Provide them with well-fitted **N95 or KN95 masks** (standard surgical or cloth masks do not filter out fine PM2.5 haze particles).\n* Pack a large bottle of water and remind them to stay hydrated throughout the day.\n* Check the school's communication channels (WhatsApp groups, Telegram, or email) before leaving, as individual private/international schools or specific districts may make last-minute calls to cancel classes.",
